          "english_quote": "As for my lineage and homeland, I do not think anyone could ever persuade me to tell them.",
          "issue": "The main rendering is defensible, but its note incorrectly presents the attachment of τινα as grammatically determined. τινα can instead be understood as the object of πεῖσαι, with the woman as the understood subject of δυνηθῆναι: she does not think she could convince anyone when stating her descent and homeland. That reading concerns the credibility of her account, rather than unwillingness to disclose it, and is contextually supported by the contrast between her present condition and her claimed noble birth.",
          "suggested_revision": "If retaining the main text, revise the note to state that it takes τινα as the subject of δυνηθῆναι and supplies the woman as the object of πεῖσαι. Preserve the alternative: “As for stating my lineage and homeland, I do not think I could ever convince anyone.” Explain that the chosen reading favors reluctance to disclose in light of her following question, while the alternative favors anticipated disbelief. Do not describe either attachment as compulsory; the revised note requires fresh review.",
